From: Tapo-C210 lean firmware
Subject: [PATCH] motor: logical-space position, measured travel, no sub-quantum moves

Three defects made the open-loop position tally diverge from reality until the
userspace clamp refused to move an axis at all.

1. invert_[xy] was applied only when encoding a move direction for the chip,
   never when reading the displacement counter back, so the tally accumulated
   in CHIP space while targets and limits live in LOGICAL space. On an
   inverted axis the two run opposite, so the tally saturated at the limit the
   axis was being driven AWAY from and the clamp then blocked exactly the
   moves that would leave it. This board runs invert_y=1 and invert_x=0 -
   hence tilt sticking at an end-stop while pan never did. Fold the inversion
   out in motor_read_position() so it always reports logical displacement.

2. The monitor thread treated "position stopped changing" as "the move
   completed" and stored the predicted target, so stalling against an
   end-stop inflated the tally. Commit the displacement the chip actually
   measured instead, by reusing motor_stop(). Zero the counter after
   committing so the monitor thread and a racing MOTOR_STOP ioctl cannot
   count the same move twice.

3. A move smaller than one position quantum encodes to target 0, which does
   not mean "stand still" - it commands the chip to drive to position 0. The
   channel never reports arrival, so the move ran until the move_timeout_ms
   watchdog fired and the displacement committed then corrupted the tally.
   Observed as an absolute/preset move needing a 13-step correction sending
   the pan axis to its minimum. Drop sub-quantum requests per axis, in the
   driver so every caller is covered (daemon chunking, homing, presets).

--- a/motor.c
+++ b/motor.c
@@ -177,15 +177,28 @@
 				stable_count++;
 				/* Consider motor stopped after 2 consecutive stable readings (200ms) */
 				if (stable_count >= 2) {
-					/* Commit pending targets when hardware reports idle */
-					motor.x_pos = motor.target_x;
-					motor.y_pos = motor.target_y;
-					motor.target_x = motor.x_pos;
-					motor.target_y = motor.y_pos;
-					motor.move_deadline_jiffies = 0;
-					pr_info("Motor stopped: x=%d, y=%d\n",
-						motor.x_pos, motor.y_pos);
-					motor.running = false;
+					/*
+					 * Commit the distance the motor ACTUALLY
+					 * travelled, not the target we predicted.
+					 *
+					 * The old code assigned motor.target_[xy]
+					 * here, i.e. it assumed every move ran to
+					 * completion. It does not: stalling against
+					 * a mechanical end-stop, or a move whose
+					 * magnitude truncates to zero in the /16
+					 * encoding, both look exactly like "position
+					 * stopped changing". Recording those as a
+					 * completed move is what made the position
+					 * tally drift away from reality until it
+					 * pinned at a limit - after which the
+					 * userspace clamp refused to move that axis
+					 * at all.
+					 *
+					 * motor_stop() already does the correct
+					 * thing (idle the channels, then add the
+					 * measured displacement), so reuse it.
+					 */
+					motor_stop();
 					stable_count = 0;
 				}
 			} else {
@@ -340,6 +353,25 @@
 
 	pr_debug("Y-axis (ch%d): raw=0x%03X, steps=%d\n", motor.y_ch, raw_value, steps);
 
+	/*
+	 * invert_[xy] is applied ONLY when encoding a move's direction for the
+	 * chip (see motor_move_steps), never on the way back. So the
+	 * displacement counter reads in CHIP space, while motor.[xy]_pos, the
+	 * targets and every limit are tracked in LOGICAL space - and on an
+	 * inverted axis the two run in opposite directions.
+	 *
+	 * Fold the inversion out here so this function always reports logical
+	 * displacement. Without this the tally for an inverted axis counts
+	 * backwards: it saturates at the limit the axis is being driven AWAY
+	 * from, so the clamp then blocks exactly the moves that would leave
+	 * that limit. This camera runs invert_y=1 and invert_x=0, which is why
+	 * tilt would stick at an end-stop and pan never did.
+	 */
+	if (invert_x)
+		*x_pos = -*x_pos;
+	if (invert_y)
+		*y_pos = -*y_pos;
+
 	mutex_unlock(&tmi8152_spi_lock);
 }
 
@@ -361,6 +393,29 @@
 	if (x_steps == 0 && y_steps == 0)
 		return;
 
+	/*
+	 * Drop any request smaller than one position quantum.
+	 *
+	 * A move is encoded as abs(steps)/TMI8152_POS_SCALE, so a sub-quantum
+	 * request encodes to target 0 - which does NOT mean "stand still", it
+	 * commands the chip to drive to position 0. The channel is started and
+	 * never reports arrival, so the move runs until the move_timeout_ms
+	 * watchdog forces a stop, and the displacement committed at that point
+	 * corrupts the position tally. (Seen as an absolute/preset move needing
+	 * a 13-step correction sending the pan axis to its minimum.)
+	 *
+	 * Guard it here rather than in the daemon so every caller is covered,
+	 * including the daemon's profiled-move chunking and the homing phases.
+	 */
+	if (abs(x_steps) < TMI8152_POS_SCALE)
+		x_steps = 0;
+	if (abs(y_steps) < TMI8152_POS_SCALE)
+		y_steps = 0;
+	if (x_steps == 0 && y_steps == 0) {
+		pr_debug("motor_move_steps: sub-quantum request dropped\n");
+		return;
+	}
+
 	/* Predict final logical coordinates so status matches daemon expectations */
 	motor.target_x = motor.x_pos;
 	motor.target_y = motor.y_pos;
@@ -652,6 +707,23 @@
 	/* Read current chip position */
 	motor_read_position(&x_pos, &y_pos);
 
+	/*
+	 * POS_L/H and PHASE_L/H are the same registers (0x08/0x09, 0x18/0x19):
+	 * a signed displacement counter that motor_move_steps() zeroes when a
+	 * move begins, so the value just read is the distance travelled by the
+	 * move that is now ending. Zero it again - the channels are idle, so
+	 * this cannot retrigger position mode - to make committing idempotent.
+	 * The monitor thread and an explicit MOTOR_STOP ioctl can both land
+	 * here for the same move; without this the displacement would be
+	 * counted twice.
+	 */
+	mutex_lock(&tmi8152_spi_lock);
+	tmi8152_spi_write(ch_phase_l[motor.x_ch] | 0x80, 0x00);
+	tmi8152_spi_write(ch_phase_h[motor.x_ch] | 0x80, 0x00);
+	tmi8152_spi_write(ch_phase_l[motor.y_ch] | 0x80, 0x00);
+	tmi8152_spi_write(ch_phase_h[motor.y_ch] | 0x80, 0x00);
+	mutex_unlock(&tmi8152_spi_lock);
+
 	/* Update position based on actual chip movement */
 	motor.x_pos += x_pos;
 	motor.y_pos += y_pos;
